        timingType: "Belt (The EA211 1.0 TFSI utilizes a robust, long-life Kevlar-reinforced timing belt system. Unlike the notorious timing chains of older VW/Audi engines, this belt drive is incredibly reliable and quiet. Audi claims a very long replacement interval, but proactive replacement around 150,000 km or 8 years is highly recommended by independent specialists to prevent catastrophic engine failure due to age-related rubber degradation).", injectionType: "Direct Injection (TFSI. Fuel is injected directly into the combustion chamber at extremely high pressure. Because it lacks secondary port injection to wash the intake valves, this engine is highly susceptible to carbon buildup over time. This carbon restricts airflow, causing rough idle and power loss, requiring physical cleaning like walnut blasting every 100,000 km).", engineLifespan: "300,000+ km (The EA211 1.0 TFSI is widely considered a highly reliable, incredibly durable little 3-cylinder engine. The lightweight aluminum block is structurally very sturdy. The engine's lifespan is mostly dictated by maintaining the cooling system and keeping up with strict oil changes to protect the small, fast-spinning turbocharger from coking).", turboType: "Single Turbocharger (Equipped with a highly responsive, electronically actuated single-scroll turbocharger that is integrated directly into the cylinder head/exhaust manifold. This ultra-compact integrated design drastically reduces turbo lag, providing peak torque of 2000 RPM, making the 3-cylinder engine feel much larger and punchier around town).", ecuType: "Bosch Motronic MED 17.5.21 (A highly sophisticated and reliable engine management unit that flawlessly handles the high-pressure direct injection, electronic wastegate actuation, and the rapid start-stop system required for modern efficiency standards).", euroStandard: "Euro 6", maxPowerStockInternals: "180+ hp (The tiny cast-aluminum block and internal components are surprisingly strong, handling increased boost very well. However, the extremely small 1.0L displacement naturally limits ultimate power figures. Stage 3 hybrid turbo upgrades are mechanically possible but are rarely cost-effective for an economy-focused powertrain).",
