        commonIssues: [ "Intake Valve Carbon Buildup: The direct injection system cannot wash the intake valves, leading to heavy carbon deposits over time. This restricts airflow, leading to cold-start misfires, rough idling, and a noticeable drop in acceleration performance.", "Water Pump & Thermostat Module Leaks: A common issue across the EA211 family. The complex plastic thermostat housing and water pump assembly is prone to warping and cracking from thousands of heat cycles, slowly weeping coolant.", "Electronic Wastegate Actuator Rattle: The linkage rod connecting the electric actuator to the turbocharger wastegate flap can develop play. This causes a distinct metallic rattling noise upon deceleration and can occasionally trigger underboost fault codes.", "High-Pressure Fuel Pump (HPFP) Noise: The cam-driven HPFP can become unusually loud, producing a prominent ticking sound. While often just a cosmetic annoyance, internal wear can eventually lead to fuel pressure drops.", "Ignition Coil Pack Failure: The extreme heat generated by the compact, integrated exhaust manifold bakes the ignition coils over time. The internal insulation degrades, causing sudden misfires and flashing check engine lights.", "Timing Belt Tensioner Squeak: While the belt itself is highly durable, the tensioner pulley bearings can sometimes dry out and produce a high-pitched squeak during cold, damp mornings." ],
        vulnerabilities: [ "Catastrophic Overheating - If the fragile plastic thermostat housing fails catastrophically or cracks open completely, the engine will dump its entire coolant capacity in seconds, instantly warping the aluminum cylinder head if the vehicle is not stopped.", "DQ200 DSG Mechatronic Failure - Models equipped with the 7-speed dry-clutch S tronic (DQ200) are highly vulnerable to mechatronic unit failure. Stop-and-go traffic overheats the unit, causing pressure loss and complete loss of gears.", "DQ200 DSG Clutch Pack Wear - The dry-clutch automatic transmission wears its clutch packs extremely fast in heavy city traffic, leading to shuddering and severe jerking during 1st to 2nd gear shifts.", "Turbocharger Bearing Failure - If the engine oil is neglected or low-quality oil is used, the tiny oil feed lines to the turbocharger will clog. The oil-starved turbo bearings will score, completely destroying the turbocharger.", "Exhaust Valve Burnout - If the engine is aggressively tuned and run hard on poor quality fuel, the resulting lean mixture and high exhaust gas temperatures can burn the edges of the exhaust valves, leading to total compression loss.", "Engine Misfires due to Carbon - Severe carbon choking on the intake valves can break off in chunks, holding the intake valves slightly open and causing a permanent loss of compression in that specific cylinder." ],
